{% sec 1, "Growing by Multiplying", "What is exponential growth, in everyday words?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Most things we count grow by adding. Save \(\text{£}10\) a week and your savings go up by the same \(\text{£}10\) every week. Plot that on a graph and you get a straight line: the same climb, step after step.</p>
    <p>{% gl "exponential growth" %}Exponential growth{% endgl %} follows a different rule. Instead of adding the same amount each step, you <em>multiply</em> by the same number each step. It sounds like a small change. It isn't.</p>
    <p>Put \(1\) penny in a jar on day one. Every day after that, double whatever is in the jar.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="tiles" aria-label="Pennies in the jar">
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 1</span><span>\(1\text{p}\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 2</span><span>\(2\text{p}\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 3</span><span>\(4\text{p}\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 7</span><span>\(64\text{p}\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 10</span><span>\(\text{£}5.12\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile"><span class="d">Day 20</span><span>\(\text{£}5{,}243\)</span></div>
    <div class="tile hot"><span class="d">Day 30</span><span>\(\text{£}5.4\text{m}\)</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For the first week the jar looks boring: \(64\text{p}\) after seven days. By day \(20\) it's over \(\text{£}5{,}000\), and by day \(30\) it's more than \(\text{£}5\) million. The rule never changed. It was "double it" every single day. What changed is the size of the thing being doubled. Doubling \(1\text{p}\) adds \(1\text{p}\). Doubling \(\text{£}1\) million adds \(\text{£}1\) million.</p>
    <p>That is the defining feature of exponential growth: <strong>how much it grows by depends on how big it already is.</strong> The bigger it gets, the faster it grows, which is why it starts slowly and then seems to explode.</p>
    <p>The same rule works in reverse. Start with \(\text{£}64\) and halve it every day: \(\text{£}32\), \(\text{£}16\), \(\text{£}8\), \(\text{£}4\), \(\text{£}2\), \(\text{£}1\), \(50\text{p}\)… It falls quickly at first, then more and more slowly. It gets very close to zero but never actually reaches it, because half of something is always still something. That's {% gl "exponential decay" %}exponential decay{% endgl %}.</p>
    <h3>Where you've already met it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Savings and investments, through {% gl "compounding" %}compounding{% endgl %}: you earn returns on your returns.</li>
      <li>Debt that isn't paid off: interest gets charged on the interest.</li>
      <li>Prices under {% gl "inflation" %}inflation{% endgl %}: a rise of a few percent a year, every year, piles up.</li>
      <li>Things that spread person to person, like a viral post or an illness.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>Adding the same amount each step gives a straight line. Multiplying by the same amount each step gives an exponential curve.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "Key Words", "What do maths and the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Base</h3><span class="tag">Maths</span></div><p>The number being multiplied over and over. It's the "multiply by this each step" number.</p><p class="eg">In \(2^{x}\) the base is \(2\): every step doubles.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Exponent</h3><span class="tag">Maths</span></div><p>Also called the power or index. It counts how many times the base has been multiplied in.</p><p class="eg">In \(2^{x}\) the exponent is \(x\). In \(2^{5} = 32\) it's \(5\).</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Exponential function</h3><span class="tag">Maths</span></div><p>A function where the variable sits up in the power. That's what makes it exponential rather than a normal power function.</p><p class="eg">\(y = 3^{x}\) is exponential. \(y = x^{3}\) is not.</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Asymptote</h3><span class="tag">Maths</span></div><p>A line a curve gets closer and closer to but never touches or crosses.</p><p class="eg">For \(y = a^{x}\) it's the \(x\)-axis, the line \(y = 0\).</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Growth factor</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>What you multiply by each period. It's the base, written in money language.</p><p class="eg">\(5\%\) growth a year means a growth factor of \(1.05\).</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Decay factor</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A growth factor below \(1\). Each period keeps only part of what was there.</p><p class="eg">Losing \(20\%\) a year means a factor of \(0.8\).</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Compounding</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Earning returns on your returns, so each period's gain becomes part of what grows next.</p><p class="eg">\(\text{£}1{,}000\) at \(10\%\): \(\text{£}1{,}100\), then \(\text{£}1{,}210\), not \(\text{£}1{,}200\).</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>CAGR</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Compound annual growth rate: the steady yearly rate that would take you from a start value to an end value.</p><p class="eg">\(\text{£}100\) to \(\text{£}150\) in \(4\) years is about \(10.7\%\) a year.</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

