{% sec 4, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate, Quant and Fintech?", "Where this shape turns up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: returns compound</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Money that earns a return each year grows exponentially, because each year's gain gets added to the pot and earns a return itself next year. If you invest \(\text{£}1{,}000\) at a return of \(r\) a year, after \(t\) years you have:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Compound growth</span>\[ V = 1000 \times (1 + r)^{t} \]</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The {% gl "growth factor" %}growth factor{% endgl %} \(1 + r\) is the base and the number of years is the exponent. Compare that with simple interest, where you only ever earn on the original \(\text{£}1{,}000\) and the line stays straight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="lab">
      <div class="lab-head"><h3>Compound vs simple</h3><p>Change the yearly return and the number of years.</p></div>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="ctrl"><label for="rate">Return a year</label><input type="range" id="rate" min="1" max="15" step="1" value="7"><output id="rate-out" for="rate">7%</output></div>
        <div class="ctrl"><label for="years">Years</label><input type="range" id="years" min="5" max="40" step="1" value="30"><output id="years-out" for="years">30</output></div>
      </div>
      <svg class="chart" id="chart-desk" viewBox="0 0 640 320" role="img" aria-label="Compound versus simple growth of 1000 pounds"></svg>
      <div class="legend"><span><i class="sw tone"></i>Compound</span><span><i class="sw grey"></i>Simple</span></div>
      <div class="readout" id="desk-read"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>This is why small differences in return matter so much over long periods. At \(7\%\) a year, \(\text{£}1{,}000\) becomes about \(\text{£}7{,}600\) after \(30\) years. At \(9\%\) it becomes about \(\text{£}13{,}300\). Two extra percentage points nearly doubles the result.</p>
      <p>It works against you too. Losses compound in the same way, which is why a \(50\%\) loss needs a \(100\%\) gain to get back to where you started: \(0.5 \times 2 = 1\).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: values, rents and debt</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Property prices and rents tend to be quoted as percentage changes, so over many years they compound in exactly the same way. Take a villa worth \(\text{£}250{,}000\) and suppose it rises \(4\%\) a year. These are made-up numbers to show the shape, not a forecast.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="numbers">
        <thead><tr><th>Years</th><th>Working</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>\(0\)</td><td>\(250{,}000\)</td><td>\(\text{£}250{,}000\)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>\(10\)</td><td>\(250{,}000 \times 1.04^{10}\)</td><td>\(\text{£}370{,}061\)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>\(20\)</td><td>\(250{,}000 \times 1.04^{20}\)</td><td>\(\text{£}547{,}781\)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The second ten years add about \(\text{£}178{,}000\), far more than the first ten years' \(\text{£}120{,}000\), even though the rate never changed. The same maths sits under a mortgage: interest is charged on what you still owe, so debt you don't pay down grows exponentially too.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Quant: decaying weights</h3><a class="theme-link tone-indigo" href="/hub/quant/">Quant</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Quants often want recent data to count more than old data. A common way to do that is to give each past day a weight that shrinks exponentially: yesterday counts a lot, last week a bit less, last month hardly at all. This is called an {% gl "ewma" %}exponentially weighted moving average{% endgl %}, and it's widely used to estimate {% gl "volatility" %}volatility{% endgl %}.</p>
      <p>The weight on a day \(k\) days ago is proportional to \(\lambda^{k}\), where \(\lambda\) (lambda) is a {% gl "decay factor" %}decay factor{% endgl %} between \(0\) and \(1\). A well-known choice for daily data is \(\lambda = 0.94\).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="lab">
      <div class="lab-head"><h3>How fast do the weights fade?</h3><p>Each bar is one past day. Move \(\lambda\) to see how quickly old days stop mattering.</p></div>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="ctrl"><label for="lam">Decay \(\lambda\)</label><input type="range" id="lam" min="0.80" max="0.99" step="0.01" value="0.94"><output id="lam-out" for="lam">0.94</output></div>
      </div>
      <svg class="chart" id="chart-ewma" viewBox="0 0 640 240" role="img" aria-label="Exponentially decaying weights over 60 past days"></svg>
      <div class="readout" id="ewma-read"></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: what inflation does to money</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Inflation is exponential decay for the value of cash. If prices rise by a rate \(\pi\) each year, what \(\text{£}1{,}000\) (or \(\text{₦}1{,}000\)) can buy shrinks like this:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Purchasing power after \(t\) years</span>\[ P = 1000 \times \left(\frac{1}{1 + \pi}\right)^{t} \]</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The base \(\frac{1}{1+\pi}\) is below \(1\), so the curve falls. At \(3\%\) inflation it takes about \(23\) years for money to lose half its buying power. At \(25\%\) it takes about \(3\) years. Nigeria's inflation has been well above \(20\%\) in recent years, which is a big part of why people look for ways to protect savings, and why payments and savings products there matter so much.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="lab">
      <div class="lab-head"><h3>How fast does cash lose value?</h3><p>Pick an inflation rate and see what \(1{,}000\) buys over \(15\) years.</p></div>
      <div class="controls">
        <div class="ctrl"><label for="infl">Inflation a year</label><input type="range" id="infl" min="1" max="40" step="1" value="25"><output id="infl-out" for="infl">25%</output></div>
      </div>
      <svg class="chart" id="chart-infl" viewBox="0 0 640 280" role="img" aria-label="Purchasing power of 1000 over 15 years"></svg>
      <div class="readout" id="infl-read"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "The Maths Underneath", "The rules, and why each one is true." %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>An exponential function has the variable in the power:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Definition</span>\[ y = a^{x}, \qquad a > 0 \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The base has to be positive. A negative base breaks down for many values of \(x\): \((-4)^{1/2}\) would be \(\sqrt{-4}\), which isn't a real number. A base of \(1\) is allowed but dull, because \(1^{x} = 1\) for every \(x\).</p>
    <h3>Six facts, and why they're true</h3>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Every graph \(y = a^{x}\) goes through \((0, 1)\).<span class="why">Because \(a^{0} = 1\) for any base. You can see why from the index laws: \(a^{0} = a^{1-1} = \frac{a}{a} = 1\).</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Each step of \(1\) in \(x\) multiplies \(y\) by \(a\).<span class="why">Because \(a^{x+1} = a^{x} \times a\). This is the "same multiplier every step" from section 1, written in symbols.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>If \(a > 1\) the graph increases, and if \(0 < a < 1\) it decreases.<span class="why">Multiplying by more than \(1\) makes things bigger each step. Multiplying by a number between \(0\) and \(1\) makes them smaller.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>\(a^{x} > 0\) for every \(x\), so the \(x\)-axis (\(y = 0\)) is an {% gl "asymptote" %}asymptote{% endgl %}.<span class="why">Multiplying or dividing a positive number by a positive number always gives a positive number. You can get as close to \(0\) as you like but never reach it.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><span>\(y = a^{-x}\) is \(y = a^{x}\) reflected in the \(y\)-axis.<span class="why">Because \(a^{-x} = \frac{1}{a^{x}} = \left(\frac{1}{a}\right)^{x}\). So \(2^{-x}\) is the same curve as \(\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^{x}\).</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">6</span><span>An exponential always overtakes any power of \(x\) in the end.<span class="why">\(2^{x}\) and \(x^{2}\) are equal at \(x = 2\) and \(x = 4\). After \(x = 4\), \(2^{x}\) pulls away for good. It even beats \(x^{10}\) eventually.</span></span></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The general model: start value times growth factor</h3>
    <p>In real problems you rarely start at \(1\). The general form puts a start value in front:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">General exponential model</span>\[ y = k \times a^{x} \qquad k = \text{value at } x = 0, \quad a = \text{growth factor} \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The compounding formula from section 4 is exactly this, with \(k = 1000\) and \(a = 1 + r\). Changing the formula changes the graph in predictable ways:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Formula</th><th>What it does to \(y = a^{x}\)</th><th>Crosses the \(y\)-axis at</th><th>Asymptote</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>\(y = k\,a^{x}\)</td><td>Stretches it vertically by \(k\)</td><td>\((0, k)\)</td><td>\(y = 0\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>\(y = a^{x} + c\)</td><td>Moves it up by \(c\)</td><td>\((0, 1 + c)\)</td><td>\(y = c\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>\(y = a^{x + c}\)</td><td>Moves it left by \(c\), same as multiplying by \(a^{c}\)</td><td>\((0, a^{c})\)</td><td>\(y = 0\)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>\(y = -a^{x}\)</td><td>Reflects it in the \(x\)-axis</td><td>\((0, -1)\)</td><td>\(y = 0\)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Working backwards: finding the growth factor</h3>
    <p>Often you know where something started and where it ended, and you want the steady rate in between. If a value goes from \(S\) to \(E\) in \(n\) steps, then \(S \times a^{n} = E\), so:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Growth factor from two values</span>\[ a = \left(\frac{E}{S}\right)^{1/n} \qquad \text{rate} = a - 1 \]</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>On the desk this is the {% gl "cagr" %}CAGR{% endgl %}, and it's how fund and company growth is usually quoted.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 1</span>
    <h3>Sketch \(y = 3^{x}\) and \(y = \left(\tfrac{1}{3}\right)^{x}\) on the same axes.</h3>
    <div class="worked-grid">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Both go through \((0, 1)\), by fact 1.</li>
        <li>\(3 > 1\), so \(y = 3^{x}\) increases. Check a couple of points: \(x = 1\) gives \(3\), \(x = -1\) gives \(\tfrac{1}{3}\).</li>
        <li>\(\tfrac{1}{3} < 1\), so \(y = \left(\tfrac{1}{3}\right)^{x}\) decreases. By fact 5 it's the first curve reflected in the \(y\)-axis.</li>
        <li>Both stay above the \(x\)-axis, which is the asymptote for each.</li>
      </ol>
      <svg class="chart" id="chart-we1" viewBox="0 0 240 200" role="img" aria-label="Sketch of y equals 3 to the x and one third to the x"></svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 2</span>
    <h3>Sketch \(y = 2^{x} + 3\). Where does it cross the \(y\)-axis, and what's the asymptote?</h3>
    <div class="worked-grid">
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>Start from \(y = 2^{x}\) and add \(3\) to every \(y\)-value. That moves the whole curve up by \(3\).</li>
        <li>The asymptote moves up with it: \(y = 3\).</li>
        <li>At \(x = 0\): \(y = 2^{0} + 3 = 1 + 3 = 4\), so it crosses at \((0, 4)\).</li>
      </ol>
      <svg class="chart" id="chart-we2" viewBox="0 0 240 200" role="img" aria-label="Sketch of y equals 2 to the x plus 3"></svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 3</span>
    <h3>A fund grows from \(\text{£}100\) to \(\text{£}150\) in \(4\) years. What steady yearly rate does that equal?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Start \(S = 100\), end \(E = 150\), steps \(n = 4\).</li>
      <li>\(a = \left(\frac{150}{100}\right)^{1/4} = 1.5^{0.25} \approx 1.1067\).</li>
      <li>Rate \(= a - 1 \approx 0.1067\), so about \(10.7\%\) a year.</li>
      <li>Check: \(100 \times 1.1067^{4} \approx 150\). ✓</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer"><strong>Why not just \(50\% \div 4 = 12.5\%\)?</strong> Because that ignores compounding. Each year's growth also grows, so the steady rate needed is lower.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 4</span>
    <h3>A curve \(y = k \times a^{x}\) passes through \((0, 5)\) and \((3, 40)\). Find \(k\) and \(a\).</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>At \(x = 0\): \(y = k \times a^{0} = k\), so \(k = 5\).</li>
      <li>At \(x = 3\): \(5 \times a^{3} = 40\), so \(a^{3} = 8\).</li>
      <li>\(a = 8^{1/3} = 2\). The curve is \(y = 5 \times 2^{x}\).</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Sketch \(y = 4^{x}\). Where does it cross the \(y\)-axis, and what is its asymptote?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>It crosses at \((0, 1)\) and the asymptote is \(y = 0\). Since \(4 > 1\), it increases, and it rises faster than \(2^{x}\): at \(x = 1\) it's already \(4\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Where does \(y = 5 \times 2^{x}\) cross the \(y\)-axis?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>At \((0, 5)\), because \(5 \times 2^{0} = 5 \times 1 = 5\). The \(k\) in front is always the starting value.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>You put \(\text{£}2{,}000\) in an account paying \(6\%\) a year, compounded yearly. How much is there after \(5\) years?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(2000 \times 1.06^{5} = 2000 \times 1.3382\ldots \approx \text{£}2{,}676.45\).</span><span>Simple interest would only give \(2000 \times (1 + 0.06 \times 5) = \text{£}2{,}600\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>A share price goes from \(\text{£}40\) to \(\text{£}90\) over \(6\) years. What is its compound annual growth rate?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(a = \left(\frac{90}{40}\right)^{1/6} = 2.25^{1/6} \approx 1.1447\), so about \(14.5\%\) a year.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>Solve \(2^{x} = 32\). Then try \(2^{x} = 20\). What goes wrong?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(2^{5} = 32\), so \(x = 5\).</span><span>For \(2^{x} = 20\) there's no whole-number answer: \(2^{4} = 16\) and \(2^{5} = 32\), so \(x\) is somewhere between \(4\) and \(5\). Finding it exactly needs logarithms, which is where this chapter goes next.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}